var definitions = ['<h2>Ce chapeau</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It is used to point out a specific object that is close to the speaker and is paired with a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>chapeau</strong> This is a French masculine singular noun meaning "hat". It is the object referred to by the demonstrative adjective "ce".</div>','<h2>Ce garçon</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this" or "that". It is used here to point out or indicate a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>garçon</strong> This is a French noun meaning "boy" or "waiter". In this context, without additional information, it most likely refers to a young male individual.</div>','<h2>Ce dîner est délicieux.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This word is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It is used here to point out a specific noun, which is "dinner" in this case.</div><div><strong>dîner</strong> This is the French word for "dinner". It is the noun being described in this sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject "ce dîner" to the adjective "délicieux".</div><div><strong>délicieux</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "delicious". Here, it is the masculine singular form used to describe the noun "dîner". If the noun were feminine, the form would be "délicieuse".</div>','<h2>Ce chat</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is the demonstrative adjective in French for "this" or "that" when referring to a masculine singular noun. It is used to point out or specify a particular noun.</div><div><strong>chat</strong> This is a French noun meaning "cat". It is masculine and singular to match with the demonstrative adjective "ce".</div>','<h2>Cette femme.</h2><div><strong>Cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, equivalent to "this" or "that" in English when referring to a feminine singular noun. It’s used to point out or specify a particular female person or object.</div><div><strong>femme</strong> This is a French noun meaning "woman" or "wife". It is feminine singular and is the object being described or pointed out by the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div>','<h2>J’adore cette guitare.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contracted form of "je" which means "I" in English, used before vowels or mute h for ease of pronunciation. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>adore</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "adorer", which means "to love" or "to adore" in English. It indicates a strong liking or enjoyment for something.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It is used to define a specific feminine singular noun.</div><div><strong>guitare</strong> This is a French noun meaning "guitar". It is feminine and singular to agree with "cette".</div>','<h2>Cette voiture</h2><div><strong>Cette</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that" when referring to a singular feminine noun. It’s used to point out or specify a particular object.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "car". It is a feminine singular noun, which is why the feminine form "cette" is used before it.</div>','<h2>Cet homme</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective for "this" or "that". It is used here to point out a specific male person or object that is close by. "Cet" is used instead of "ce" before a masculine noun that begins with a vowel or mute h.</div><div><strong>homme</strong> This is the French noun for "man". It’s in the masculine singular form. In this context, it is the noun that "cet" is modifying, specifying the particular person being referred to.</div>','<h2>Il y a des pommes dans cet arbre.</h2><div><strong>Il y a</strong> This is a French phrase that translates to "there is" or "there are" in English. It is used to indicate the existence of something. In this sentence, it’s introducing the fact that apples are present.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is a French partitive article used before a plural noun to mean "some" in English. It indicates an unspecified quantity of apples.</div><div><strong>pommes</strong> This is the French word for "apples". It’s a plural noun in this context.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a French preposition meaning "in". It indicates location or position in this sentence.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It modifies a masculine singular noun that starts with a vowel or silent "h", indicating the noun is near the speaker.</div><div><strong>arbre</strong> This is the French word for "tree". It’s a masculine singular noun and is the object of the preposition "dans" in this sentence.</div>','<h2>Cet avion en papier</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective for masculine singular nouns that begin with a vowel or mute "h". It is equivalent to "this" in English and is used to point out a specific object that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>avion</strong> This is a noun in French that means "airplane" or "plane". It is masculine and in this context, it refers to a model or toy plane rather than a full-sized aircraft.</div><div><strong>en</strong> In this context, "en" is a French preposition commonly used to express the material something is made from. It translates to "made of" or "in" when referring to materials in English.</div><div><strong>papier</strong> This is a noun in French meaning "paper". It is the material from which the plane mentioned in this sentence is made.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>Je voudrais déposer ce chèque.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>voudrais</strong> This is the conditional form of the French verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It expresses a polite desire or request, similar to "would like" in English.</div><div><strong>déposer</strong> This is a French verb that means "to deposit". In this sentence, it’s in the infinitive form because it follows a modal verb (voudrais).</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this". It precedes a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>chèque</strong> This is a French noun meaning "check" (a form of payment). It is masculine singular, which is why "ce" is used as the demonstrative adjective.</div>','<h2>Vous avez bu un café ce matin.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here to address either one person formally or multiple people.</div><div><strong>avez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural of the present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used to form the compound past.</div><div><strong>bu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "boire", which means "to drink". In the compound past, it is used in conjunction with the auxiliary verb "avoir" to indicate a completed action.</div><div><strong>un café</strong> This translates to "a coffee" in English. "Un" is the indefinite masculine singular article, and "café" means coffee.</div><div><strong>ce matin</strong> This phrase means "this morning" in English. "Ce" is a demonstrative adjective, and "matin" means morning.</div>','<h2>Elle lira ce livre rapidement.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lira</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It indicates a future action that she will perform.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, equivalent to "this" in English. It is used here to point out a specific object, which is masculine singular due to the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French meaning "book". It is the object of the verb "lira" and is what she will read.</div><div><strong>rapidement</strong> This is an adverb in French, which translates to "quickly" in English. It describes the manner in which she will read the book.</div>','<h2>D’abord, je finirai ce projet.</h2><div><strong>D’abord</strong> This French adverb means "firstly" or "to begin with". It indicates that the action of completing the project will be the first task or priority.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>finirai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense of the verb "finir", which means "to finish" or "to complete". It indicates the speaker’s intention to finish something in the future.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It is used here to denote a specific project that is close to the speaker, in terms of attention or relevance.</div><div><strong>projet</strong> This is a French noun meaning "project". It is the direct object of the verb "finirai", referring to the task that the speaker intends to complete.</div>','<h2>J’ai dû me réveiller tôt ce matin.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of the French first-person singular pronoun "je" and the following vowel, used here to avoid the hiatus between two vowel sounds. The full form is "je" which means "I" in English.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it’s used to form the past tense known as the passé composé.</div><div><strong>dû</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". The circumflex accent over the "u" indicates that it is the past form. In this sentence, it conveys the sense of obligation in the past.</div><div><strong>me</strong> This is the first-person singular reflexive pronoun, used here to indicate that the subject performs the action on themselves. It’s the object of the verb that follows.</div><div><strong>réveiller</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to wake up". In this context, used reflexively, it means "to wake oneself up".</div><div><strong>tôt</strong> This is an adverb meaning "early" in English. It modifies the verb to indicate the time of the action.</div><div><strong>ce matin</strong> This phrase consists of the demonstrative adjective "ce" (this) and the noun "matin" (morning), which together mean "this morning".</div>','<h2>Il devait m’appeler ce matin.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devait</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". In this context, it implies an obligation in the past or something that was supposed to happen – "was supposed to" or "should have".</div><div><strong>m’appeler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "appeler" preceded by "me", which is an object pronoun meaning "me" in English. When combined, "m’appeler" means "to call me".</div><div><strong>ce matin</strong> This phrase means "this morning" in English. "Ce" is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this", and "matin" is a noun meaning "morning".</div>','<h2>Merci. Combien de temps dure ce vol?</h2><div><strong>Merci</strong> This is the French word for "thank you". It is commonly used to express gratitude.</div><div><strong>Combien</strong> This translates to "how much" or "how many" in English. It is used to ask for a quantity or degree of something.</div><div><strong>de temps</strong> This phrase means "of time" when translated. "Temps" is the French word for "time", and "de" is a preposition that here means "of".</div><div><strong>dure</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "durer", which means "to last" or "to endure". It tells us how long an action lasts.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It precedes and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "vol" (flight).</div><div><strong>vol</strong> This is a French noun meaning "flight". It is masculine and singular in form here.</div>','<h2>Ce magasin vend des jouets très amusants.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this" or "that". It is used here to point out a specific store or shop.</div><div><strong>magasin</strong> This is a noun in French that means "store" or "shop". It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating the place where the action of selling is taking place.</div><div><strong>vend</strong> This word is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "vendre", which means "to sell" in English. It indicates the action that the store is performing.</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is an indefinite plural article in French, equivalent to "some" in English. It precedes the plural noun "jouets" to indicate that there is more than one toy, and their exact quantity is not specified.</div><div><strong>jouets</strong> This is a plural noun that means "toys" in English. It is the direct object of the verb "vend", specifying what the store is selling.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is an adverb that means "very" in English. It’s used here to intensify the adjective that follows it.</div><div><strong>amusants</strong> This is the plural masculine form of the adjective "amusant", which means "fun" or "amusing" in English. It describes the toys being sold and agrees in number and gender with the noun it modifies.</div>','<h2>J’ai choisi d’acheter ce chandail mauve.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is a contraction of "je", the first-person singular pronoun in French, meaning "I". It contracts to "j’" because the following word begins with a vowel, making it easier to pronounce.</div><div><strong>ai</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it functions as an auxiliary verb to form the past compound tense.</div><div><strong>choisi</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "choisir", which means "to choose". It is used with the auxiliary verb "avoir" to form the past tense, indicating a completed action.</div><div><strong>d’acheter</strong> This is the infinitive verb "acheter" preceded by "de", which together means "to buy". The "de" is required after the verb "choisir" when it is followed by an infinitive verb.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It is used here to introduce and modify the noun "chandail", indicating a specific item.</div><div><strong>chandail</strong> This is a French word for "sweater". In this sentence, it is the direct object noun that is being bought.</div><div><strong>mauve</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "purple" or "mauve" in English. Adjectives in French typically follow the nouns they modify, and this one describes the color of the sweater.</div>','<h2>Je ne veux jamais oublier ce moment.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is part of the negation in French. Together with "pas", "jamais", or other negative expressions, it negates a verb. Here it is used in the construction of negation with "jamais", which means "never".</div><div><strong>veux</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "vouloir", which means "to want". It expresses a desire or will.</div><div><strong>jamais</strong> This is the French word for "never". When used with "ne", it negates the action of the verb to mean "never" as in "I never want".</div><div><strong>oublier</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "oublier", which means "to forget".</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this". It precedes a noun to indicate a specific thing.</div><div><strong>moment</strong> This is a French noun meaning "moment" or "instant". Here, it is the direct object of the verb "oublier", which is the thing the speaker does not want to forget.</div>','<h2>Ils se sont amusés en camping ce week-end.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural subject pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It is the subject of the sentence, referring to a group of people.</div><div><strong>se sont amusés</strong> This phrase is a reflexive verb in the past tense (le passé composé). "Se" indicates that the action is reflexive, meaning the subjects are performing the action on themselves. "Sont" is the auxiliary verb "être" in the third-person plural, used here to form the past tense. "Amusés" is the past participle of "amuser", which means "to have fun" or "to enjoy oneself". The ending "-és" indicates that the subjects are male or a mixed group. If the group were all female, it would be "amusées".</div><div><strong>en</strong> This preposition in French can mean "in", "on", or "while". In this sentence, it is used to indicate the circumstance or the activity during which they had fun, which is camping.</div><div><strong>camping</strong> This is a French noun that means the same as in English, referring to the activity of spending time outdoors in a tent, camper, etc. It is used here directly after "en" to describe the activity they did.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that", which precedes and agrees with masculine singular nouns.</div><div><strong>week-end</strong> Borrowed from English, "week-end" means the same in French and refers to the end of the week, typically Saturday and Sunday. The hyphen is maintained in the French spelling.</div>','<h2>Oui. Ce serait vraiment bon.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It’s an affirmative response to a statement or a question.</div><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, equivalent to "this" or "that" in English. Here, it refers to a situation or a thing previously mentioned or understood from context.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditionnel present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". The conditionnel mood is used to express hypothetical situations or polite requests.</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is an adverb meaning "really" or "truly" in English. It’s used to emphasize the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>bon</strong> This is an adjective meaning "good". It can refer to the quality of something being positive or favourable. In this case, it is masculine singular to agree with "ce". If it were referring to a feminine noun, it would be "bonne".</div>','<h2>Ce chien est très gros.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that". It’s used here to point out a specific noun, which is "chien" (dog).</div><div><strong>chien</strong> This is the French word for "dog". It’s a masculine singular noun and the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes the state of being of the subject, "chien".</div><div><strong>très</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "very". It’s used here to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>gros</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "big" or "fat". Here, it describes the size or weight of the dog. It is in the masculine singular form to agree with the noun "chien".</div>','<h2>Oui, ce serait bien, merci.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is an affirmative response to a statement or question.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French. In English, it is translated as "this" or "it" depending on the context. It is used here to refer to something previously mentioned or understood in the conversation.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense of the verb "être", which means "to be" in English. In this context, it translates to "would be", indicating a hypothetical or polite expression.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This word translates to "good" or "well" in English. Here it is used as an adverb to describe how something would be.</div><div><strong>merci</strong> This is the French word for "thank you". It’s an expression of gratitude.</div>','<h2>Je ne connais pas ce genre de livres.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is the negation particle used in French to form a negative construction. Together with "pas", it negates the verb of the sentence.</div><div><strong>connais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "connaître", which means "to know" or "to be familiar with". It indicates that the subject has knowledge or is acquainted with someone or something.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This is the second part of the negation that in conjunction with "ne" negates the verb. It is placed after the conjugated verb in the sentence.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It is used to indicate a specific noun that is close in time or space.</div><div><strong>genre</strong> This is a noun which means "type" or "kind" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition that in this context means "of" in English. It is used to link the noun "genre" with "livres" to indicate the kind of books.</div><div><strong>livres</strong> This is the plural form of the noun "livre", which means "books" in English.</div>','<h2>Elle lit encore ce livre merveilleux.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lit</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "lire", which means "to read". It describes the action performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>encore</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "still" or "again". It indicates that the action of reading the book is continuing or being repeated.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It modifies the noun that follows it, making it more specific.</div><div><strong>livre</strong> This is a French noun meaning "book". Here, it is the direct object of the verb "lit" (reads), indicating what she is reading.</div><div><strong>merveilleux</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "wonderful" or "marvelous". It qualifies the noun "livre" (book), indicating that the book is extraordinary or delightful. The adjective agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes; if the book were feminine, it would be "merveilleuse".</div>','<h2>Il y a de belles chemises dans ce magasin.</h2><div><strong>Il y a</strong> This is a common French expression that translates to "there is" or "there are" in English. It is used to indicate the existence or presence of something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" translates to "some" in English. It is an indefinite article that is used before an adjective that comes before a plural noun.</div><div><strong>belles</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the French adjective "beau", which means "beautiful". Since "chemises" is a feminine plural noun, the adjective "belles" agrees in both gender and number.</div><div><strong>chemises</strong> This is the French word for "shirts". It is a feminine plural noun.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "in" when indicating location.</div><div><strong>ce</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It is used before a masculine singular noun to indicate a specific thing.</div><div><strong>magasin</strong> This is the French word for "store" or "shop". It is a masculine singular noun.</div>','<h2>Ce serait une bonne motivation pour parler français.</h2><div><strong>Ce</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun in French, roughly equivalent to "this" or "it" in English, often used to refer to a previously mentioned idea or to introduce a new one.</div><div><strong>serait</strong> This is the third-person singular conditional tense of the verb "être", which means "to be". It suggests a possibility or hypothetical scenario, equivalent to "would be" in English.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This corresponds to the English indefinite article "a" for feminine singular nouns. It precedes the noun "motivation" here.</div><div><strong>bonne</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "bon", which means "good". It modifies the noun "motivation" to express quality.</div><div><strong>motivation</strong> This is a feminine noun in French meaning "motivation". It signifies an incentive or drive to do something.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "for". It’s used to indicate the purpose of the motivation.</div><div><strong>parler</strong> This is an infinitive verb meaning "to speak" or "to talk" in English. It indicates the action related to the motivation.</div><div><strong>français</strong> This is a noun and also an adjective meaning "French". As a noun, it refers to the French language. In this sentence, it describes the language that the speaker could be motivated to speak.</div>','<h2>Est-ce que cette boîte est la tienne?</h2><div><strong>Est-ce que</strong> This is a phrase used to form questions in French. It literally translates to "is it that" but is more generally understood as a way to indicate that a question is being asked, similar to the English word "is" at the beginning of a question.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that" in the feminine singular form. It is used to specify a particular noun that is close to the speaker, in this case, "boîte" (box).</div><div><strong>boîte</strong> This is a French noun meaning "box". It is feminine singular to match the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this question, it’s used to establish the relationship between "cette boîte" (this box) and "la tienne" (yours).</div><div><strong>la</strong> In this context, "la" is the feminine singular definite article "the" in English, used before the possessive "tienne".</div><div><strong>tienne</strong> This is a possessive pronoun in French that means "yours". It is the feminine singular form to agree with "boîte" and refers to something owned by the person being addressed. The masculine singular form would be "le tien".</div>','<h2>Tu chantes très bien cette chanson.</h2><div><strong>Tu</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English when speaking informally to one person. It serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>chantes</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "chanter", which means "to sing". It describes the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>très</strong> This word translates to "very" in English. It is an adverb used to enhance the adverb "bien".</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "well". It describes the manner in which the subject is singing.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It refers to a specific, feminine singular noun that follows it, which in this case is "chanson".</div><div><strong>chanson</strong> This is a French noun meaning "song". It is the object of the sentence and is feminine singular, which is why the preceding demonstrative adjective "cette" is used.</div>','<h2>Oui. Je ne comprends pas cette question.</h2><div><strong>Oui</strong> This is the French word for "yes". It is often used to affirm a statement or respond positively to a question.</div><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ne</strong> This is part of a negation phrase in French. Along with "pas", it surrounds a verb to negate a statement, translating to "not" in English.</div><div><strong>comprends</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "comprendre", which means "to understand". It describes an action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>pas</strong> This word completes the negation phrase begun by "ne". Together, "ne...pas" negates the meaning of the verb it surrounds, comparable to "do not" in English.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this". It is used to refer to a singular feminine noun that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>question</strong> This is a feminine noun in French meaning "question". It’s the object of the verb "comprends" and the entity being discussed in the sentence.</div>','<h2>Ouais, je vais t’appeler cette semaine.</h2><div><strong>Ouais</strong> This is an informal French interjection equivalent to "yeah" or "yep" in English, which is often used to show agreement or acknowledgment.</div><div><strong>je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vais</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aller", which means "to go" in English. However, in this context, it’s part of the near future construction "vais + infinitive" which translates to "I’m going to" do something, in this case, "I’m going to call".</div><div><strong>t’appeler</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "appeler" preceded by the object pronoun "te" which is contracted to "t’ " before a vowel. Altogether, it means "to call you".</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this" in English. It is used here to modify "semaine" and agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes.</div><div><strong>semaine</strong> This is a feminine noun meaning "week" in English.</div>','<h2>Présentez cette ordonnance à la pharmacie.</h2><div><strong>Présentez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural imperative form of the verb "présenter", which means "to present" or "to submit". It’s directed towards the listener, instructing them to take action.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the demonstrative adjective "this" or "that" in English. It is used here to refer to a specific prescription ("ordonnance"), which is feminine in French.</div><div><strong>ordonnance</strong> This is a French noun meaning "prescription". It’s a formal authorization from a medical professional for a patient to be issued a medicine or treatment.</div><div><strong>à</strong> This is a preposition in French that here means "to" or "at" in English. It indicates the direction or destination of the action (presenting the prescription).</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article for feminine singular nouns in French, equivalent to "the" in English. It specifies the pharmacy that the prescription should be presented to.</div><div><strong>pharmacie</strong> This is a French noun meaning "pharmacy" or "drugstore". It’s the place where medicines are dispensed and medical advice can be obtained.</div>','<h2>Elle louera cette voiture pour une semaine.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>louera</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "louer", which means "to rent". In this sentence, it indicates a future action where she will rent something.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this" in English. It’s used to specify the noun that follows it, which is feminine singular due to the noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a French noun meaning "car". It is in the singular form and has a feminine gender, which agrees with the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This translates to "for" in English. It is a preposition used to indicate purpose or duration.</div><div><strong>une</strong> This is the indefinite article in French, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. In this sentence, it is used to specify "une semaine" meaning one week, denoting the duration of the rental.</div><div><strong>semaine</strong> This is a French noun meaning "week". It indicates the time period for which the car will be rented.</div>','<h2>Cette soupe est meilleure que la dernière fois.</h2><div><strong>Cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this" or "that". It is used here to specify a particular soup and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is feminine singular.</div><div><strong>soupe</strong> This is a French noun meaning "soup". It is the subject of the sentence and is feminine singular, matching with the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It serves as a linking verb in this sentence, connecting the subject "cette soupe" to its descriptor.</div><div><strong>meilleure</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "meilleur", which means "better". It is used to compare the quality of the soup to that of a previous time. In this sentence, it serves as the comparative form of "bon" (good).</div><div><strong>que</strong> This is a French conjunction meaning "than". It is used in comparative structures to introduce the reference point of the comparison.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the definite article in French meaning "the". It is used here to specify the particular occasion being compared to the current one and agrees with "fois" in gender and number.</div><div><strong>dernière</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the adjective "dernier", which means "last". It qualifies the noun "fois" indicating the most recent occurrence being compared to.</div><div><strong>fois</strong> This is a French noun meaning "time" or "occasion". In this sentence, it refers to the previous instance when the soup was tasted.</div>','<h2>N’importe qui peut chanter cette chanson drôle.</h2><div><strong>N’importe qui</strong> This French phrase means "anyone" or "whoever". It suggests that there are no restrictions on who the subject could be.</div><div><strong>peut</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "pouvoir", which means "to be able to" or "can". It indicates the ability of the subject to perform an action.</div><div><strong>chanter</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to sing" in French. It is the main action that the subject can perform.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French that means "this". It is used to indicate a specific, singular, feminine noun that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>chanson</strong> This is a feminine noun that means "song" in French.</div><div><strong>drôle</strong> This is a French adjective that means "funny". In this sentence, it modifies the noun "chanson" and agrees in gender and number, since "chanson" is feminine and singular.</div>','<h2>Ils ont lu cette histoire des douzaines de fois.</h2><div><strong>Ils</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "they" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>ont</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the auxiliary verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this context, it is used to form the past tense (called the passé composé).</div><div><strong>lu</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "lire", which means "to read". Combined with "ont", it forms the passé composé, indicating that the action of reading has been completed in the past.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this". It is feminine singular to agree with the noun "histoire" (story).</div><div><strong>histoire</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French meaning "story".</div><div><strong>des</strong> This is the plural indefinite article in French, used here to introduce "douzaines de fois". It is a combination of "de" (of) and "les" (the). When combined with "douzaines", it translates as "dozens of".</div><div><strong>douzaines</strong> This is a feminine plural noun meaning "dozens".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in French. In this context, it is part of the phrase "des douzaines de fois" and means "of", creating a meaning similar to "times" as in "occasions" or "instances".</div><div><strong>fois</strong> This is a feminine plural noun meaning "times". It is used here to indicate the number of times the story was read.</div>','<h2>D’accord, peu importe, j’aime bien cette couleur.</h2><div><strong>D’accord</strong> This is a French expression meaning "okay" or "alright". It indicates agreement or acknowledgement.</div><div><strong>peu importe</strong> This phrase translates to "it doesn’t matter" or "never mind". It’s used to dismiss the importance of something or to express indifference.</div><div><strong>j’aime</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". In this context, it indicates a personal preference or affection for something.</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This word can serve various functions, but here it serves as an adverb to enhance the verb "aimer", making it "quite like" or "really like".</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the demonstrative adjective, which means "this" in English. It points out a specific thing that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>couleur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "color". It is feminine singular to match the demonstrative adjective "cette".</div>','<h2>Selon cette radiographie, tout va bien.</h2><div><strong>Selon</strong> This is a French preposition that means "according to". It is used to refer to the source of information or the basis of a statement.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the demonstrative adjective "ce", meaning "this" in English. It’s used to specify which radiograph (x-ray) is being referred to and agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is "radiographie".</div><div><strong>radiographie</strong> This is a French noun that means "radiography" or "x-ray". It’s a feminine noun, which is why "cette" is used. In this sentence, it is the subject of the clause.</div><div><strong>tout</strong> This is a French word that means "everything" or "all". In this sentence, it is used to say that everything is fine or in good order.</div><div><strong>va</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the French verb "aller", which means "to go". In this context, it is used idiomatically to mean that everything is functioning well or is in a good state (everything goes well).</div><div><strong>bien</strong> This is an adverb meaning "well" in English. It complements the verb "va", enhancing the meaning to convey that all is well according to the x-ray results.</div>','<h2>Il y a quelque chose de lourd dans cette boîte.</h2><div><strong>Il</strong> This is the impersonal pronoun "it" in English. In this construction, it is used as part of the expression "il y a", which means "there is" or "there are".</div><div><strong>y a</strong> This comes from the verb "avoir" which means "to have". In the expression "il y a", it is equivalent to "there is" or "there are" in English, indicating the existence or presence of something.</div><div><strong>quelque chose</strong> This phrase means "something" in English. It’s a pronoun used to refer to an unspecified object or thing.</div><div><strong>de</strong> In this context, "de" is a preposition used in French to construct a description, here it means "of" or "from" in English, linking "quelque chose" (something) to the adjective that describes it.</div><div><strong>lourd</strong> This is an adjective meaning "heavy" in English. It describes the noun, implying that the item has significant weight.</div><div><strong>dans</strong> This is a preposition that means "in" in English. It’s used to indicate the location or position of something.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It specifies which box is being talked about, indicating it’s the one that’s near or being referred to in the conversation.</div><div><strong>boîte</strong> This word means "box" in English. It’s a feminine noun that is the object of the preposition "dans", telling us where the heavy item is.</div>','<h2>J’ai échangé ma vieille voiture contre cette nouvelle.</h2><div><strong>J’</strong> This is the contraction of "je" (first-person singular pronoun in French, "I" in English) with the following word that starts with a vowel or mute h, in this case "ai", to make it easier to pronounce.</div><div><strong>ai échangé</strong> This is the first-person singular past tense conjugation of the verb "échanger", which means "to exchange" or "to trade". "Ai" is from the auxiliary verb "avoir", used here to construct the passé composé, a common past tense in French.</div><div><strong>ma</strong> This is the possessive adjective in French, the feminine singular form of "my" in English, agreeing with the feminine noun "voiture".</div><div><strong>vieille</strong> This is an adjective in French that means "old". It agrees in gender and number with the feminine singular noun that it describes, "voiture".</div><div><strong>voiture</strong> This is a feminine singular noun in French meaning "car" or "vehicle".</div><div><strong>contre</strong> This is a preposition in French meaning "against" or "in exchange for" in this context. It indicates the action of trading one thing for another.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is the demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this" in English. It agrees in gender and number with the feminine singular noun it precedes, "nouvelle".</div><div><strong>nouvelle</strong> This is an adjective meaning "new". Like "vieille", it agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is omitted but understood (in this case, "nouvelle voiture").</div>','<h2>Elle portera cette robe mauve ce soir.</h2><div><strong>Elle</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, referring to a female individual.</div><div><strong>portera</strong> This is the future simple tense of the verb "porter", which means "to wear". It indicates that the subject will wear something in the future.</div><div><strong>cette</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French, equivalent to "this" in English. It’s used to point out a specific object that is close to the speaker, and it agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which is feminine singular here.</div><div><strong>robe</strong> This is a French noun meaning "dress". It is a feminine singular noun.</div><div><strong>mauve</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "mauve", a color between purple and pink. In French, adjectives usually follow the noun they describe and must agree in gender and number with the noun.</div><div><strong>ce soir</strong> This phrase means "this evening" or "tonight" in English. It is an expression of time and sets the context for when the action of the verb will take place.</div>','<h2>Que penses-tu de cet endroit?</h2><div><strong>Que</strong> This is the French interrogative word for "what". It is used to introduce a question.</div><div><strong>penses-tu</strong> This is a combination of the verb "penser" meaning "to think" and the subject pronoun "tu" meaning "you" (singular familiar). Together in the form "penses-tu", it constructs the question "do you think" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is the French preposition meaning "of" or "about". It is used here to link the verb "penser" to the object of the thought.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It’s the masculine singular form used before a vowel or mute "h" and modifies the noun "endroit".</div><div><strong>endroit</strong> This is a masculine noun in French that means "place" or "spot". In this sentence, it’s the object of discussion, the "place" that someone is being asked their opinion about.</div>','<h2>Cet aspirateur est le nôtre.</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective for "this" or "that", used here to point out a specific object. It agrees in gender and number with the noun it modifies, which in this case is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>aspirateur</strong> This is a French noun meaning "vacuum cleaner". It is masculine singular to match the demonstrative adjective.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". In this sentence, it connects the subject "Cet aspirateur" with the predicate "le nôtre".</div><div><strong>le</strong> This article is the French definite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes and agrees with "nôtre" to reinforce the idea that it is specifically our vacuum cleaner being discussed.</div><div><strong>nôtre</strong> This is a possessive pronoun meaning "ours" in English. It shows ownership and replaces "aspirateur" to avoid repetition. As with "le", it is also masculine singular to match the noun it stands for.</div>','<h2>Cet arbre est vraiment grand.</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English when referring to a masculine singular noun that starts with a vowel or mute "h". It is used to point out a specific thing or person that is close to the speaker.</div><div><strong>arbre</strong> This is the French word for "tree". It is a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>est</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It links the subject of the sentence (arbre) to its descriptor (grand).</div><div><strong>vraiment</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "really" or "truly" in English. It is used to emphasize the adjective that follows it.</div><div><strong>grand</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "big" or "tall" in English. It is in the masculine singular form to agree with the noun "arbre". If the noun were feminine, it would be "grande".</div>','<h2>Je sauterai en bas de cet avion.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sauterai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense form of the verb "sauter", which means "to jump". It indicates a future action that the subject plans to carry out.</div><div><strong>en bas de</strong> This is a prepositional phrase in French that translates to "down from" in English. It indicates the direction of the jump.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is the demonstrative adjective in French meaning "this", used before a masculine singular noun that begins with a vowel sound. It specifies the noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>avion</strong> This is a masculine noun in French that means "airplane". In this sentence, it indicates the object from which the subject is planning to jump.</div>','<h2>Nous écouterons souvent cet album.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>écouterons</strong> This is the first-person plural future tense form of the verb "écouter", which means "to listen". This form is used to express an action that will take place often in the future.</div><div><strong>souvent</strong> This is a French adverb meaning "often". It describes the frequency with which the action of the verb "écouter" will be carried out.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that". It is used to point out a specific noun that is masculine and singular, and it precedes a word that begins with a vowel or mute "h".</div><div><strong>album</strong> This is a French noun meaning "album". It is the object of the sentence, the thing that will be listened to often.</div>','<h2>Vous tondrez le gazon cet après-midi.</h2><div><strong>Vous</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "you" in English. It is used here to address either one person formally or multiple people.</div><div><strong>tondrez</strong> This is the second-person formal or plural future tense form of the verb "tondre", which means "to mow". In this sentence, it indicates an action that will be undertaken by "you" in the future.</div><div><strong>le gazon</strong> This phrase translates to "the lawn" or "the grass" in English. "Le" is the definite article, used here because it’s referring to a specific area of grass that is to be mowed.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this". It precedes masculine singular nouns that begin with a vowel or silent "h" and it is used here to specify the time frame.</div><div><strong>après-midi</strong> This translates to "afternoon" in English. It is a masculine noun used here to specify when the action of mowing the lawn will take place.</div>','<h2>Nous détesterons étudier pour cet examen.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>détesterons</strong> This is the future tense form of the verb "détester", which means "to hate". Here, it indicates that the action of hating will occur in the future.</div><div><strong>étudier</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "étudier", which means "to study". In this sentence, it serves as the object of hate constituted by "détesterons".</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition that means "for". It indicates the purpose of the studying.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is the masculine singular form of the demonstrative adjective "ce", which means "this". It precedes the masculine singular noun "examen".</div><div><strong>examen</strong> This is a French noun meaning "exam" or "test". It’s the object that the studying is being directed towards.</div>','<h2>Nous avons loué cet appartement pour un an.</h2><div><strong>Nous</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in French, equivalent to "we" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>avons</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "avoir", which means "to have". In this sentence, it is used as an auxiliary verb to form the compound past.</div><div><strong>loué</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "louer", which means "to rent". Combined with the auxiliary "avons", it indicates a past action of renting.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in French that means "this". It is the masculine singular form used before a word starting with a vowel or silent "h".</div><div><strong>appartement</strong> This is a French noun meaning "apartment".</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a preposition in French that means "for" in the context of indicating a duration or purpose.</div><div><strong>un an</strong> This is a French phrase meaning "one year". "Un" is the masculine indefinite article "a" or "an", and "an" means "year". The phrase indicates the duration of the rental.</div>','<h2>Je devrai faire la lessive cet après-midi.</h2><div><strong>Je</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in French, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>devrai</strong> This is the first-person singular future tense of the verb "devoir", which means "to have to" or "must". Hence, "devrai" translates to "will have to" or "will need to" indicate a future obligation.</div><div><strong>faire</strong> This is the infinitive form of the verb "to do" or "to make" in English. In this sentence, it follows "devrai" and expresses the action that needs to be done, which is "to do" the laundry.</div><div><strong>la lessive</strong> This is a French noun meaning "laundry". In this context, "la lessive" refers to the clothes that need to be washed or the act of washing them.</div><div><strong>cet</strong> This is the French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" in English. It precedes and agrees with masculine singular nouns that begin with a vowel or mute h, like "après-midi".</div><div><strong>après-midi</strong> This French noun means "afternoon". It’s a masculine singular noun despite ending in "i", which is atypical in French. It tells when the action of doing the laundry is intended to take place.</div>','<h2>Cet ordinateur est parfait pour ceux qui aiment les jeux.</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is the demonstrative adjective in French, meaning "this" or "that". It is used here to denote a specific computer, and it agrees with the masculine singular noun it modifies.</div><div><strong>ordinateur</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in French that means "computer".</div><div><strong>est</strong> This word is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It is used to link the subject "cet ordinateur" with its complement.</div><div><strong>parfait</strong> This is an adjective in French that means "perfect". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is masculine singular here.</div><div><strong>pour</strong> This is a French preposition that means "for". It is used to indicate the intended purpose or recipient of something.</div><div><strong>ceux</strong> This is the masculine plural relative pronoun in French, meaning "those". It refers to people who are characterized by the following relative clause.</div><div><strong>qui</strong> This is a relative pronoun in French that means "who" or "which". It introduces a relative clause, giving information about the noun it follows.</div><div><strong>aiment</strong> This is the third-person plural present tense form of the verb "aimer", which means "to like" or "to love". It describes the action taken by the people referred to by "ceux".</div><div><strong>les</strong> This is the definite article in French, used in the plural form here. It means "the" and is used to specify the noun it precedes.</div><div><strong>jeux</strong> This is a masculine plural noun in French that means "games". It’s the object of affection of those who "aiment" them.</div>','<h2>Cet examen était facile.</h2><div><strong>Cet</strong> This is a French demonstrative adjective meaning "this" or "that" in English. It’s used to indicate a specific noun that is close to the speaker and is masculine singular, matching the noun "examen" which is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>examen</strong> This is a French noun meaning "exam" or "test" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>était</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "être", which means "to be". It describes a state of being or condition in the past.</div><div><strong>facile</strong> This is a French adjective meaning "easy". It is describing the noun "examen" and agrees in number and gender, hence no additional "e" at the end as "examen" is masculine and singular.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);